  "metadata": {
    "type": "targeted_grid",
    "name": "knots_method_pilot",
    "description": "Knots Method Pilot: legacy scripted partition vs real RDTS soft fork\n\nPURPOSE: Before any large real-fork sweep, check whether the legacy\nasymmetric-softfork method (partition_miner_with_pools.py: scripted\nisland partition, probabilistic block acceptance, accounting-only\nswitching) produces results similar to the properly modeled soft fork\n(knots_mesh_pilot.py: real Knots nodes rejecting real RDTS-violating\nblocks over ordinary P2P relay, optionally with nodes switching software\nin place). Every scenario in this spec is run once per method from the\nsame build manifest, so the only difference between runs of a scenario is\nthe fork mechanism.\n\nMETHODS (3_run_sweep.py --method; one runner per method, separate results dirs):\n  1. legacy                                 prior studies' method\n  2. realfork --oracle-chain-source mined   real fork, oracles fed the same\n                                            mined-block counts as legacy\n  3. realfork                               real fork, oracles read observed\n                                            chain state (surviving blocks)\n  4. inplace                                3 + pools and economic/user nodes\n                                            change what their node enforces\nEach step changes one thing. Agreement between 1 and 2 validates the legacy\nmethod as a model of the fork itself; 2 -> 3 isolates the oracle input\nchange; 3 -> 4 isolates real node-runner/pool software switching.\n\nDESIGN:\n- 3 violation_rate values x 2 seeds = 6 scenarios, x 4 methods = 24 runs\n- Network: full (realistic-economy-v2, 60 nodes)\n- Fixed parameters: chainsplit_persistence's (E=0.55, hashrate_split=0.25,\n  ideology 0.51, max_loss 0.26, neutral 30%), at its maximum-pain\n  commitment point C=0.30 (Foundry committed to v27; the most contested\n  regime, where a mechanism difference should show first)\n- fork_links=5 (the knots-mesh-pilot count; real-fork methods only; legacy\n  keeps its two-island topology)\n- Run settings: configs/knots_method_pilot_run.yaml (13000s, 10s interval,\n  2016-block retarget; the full-network baseline)\n\nKEY VIOLATION RATES (= 1 - v26_acceptance_probability; legacy gets p):\n- 0.20: chainsplit_persistence's startup-failure regime at C=0.30\n- 0.50: moderate\n- 1.00: strict (every Core block violates), the prior baseline\n\nfork_heal_exit is OFF for all methods. Under the real fork a heal can be\ntransient: Core reorgs onto the Knots chain, then its next block re-includes\nthe pending violating txs and the split re-forms. Exiting at the first heal\nwould cut real-fork runs short. Heal time is still recorded\n(fork_convergence), and real-fork runs also record chain_state and outcomes.\n\nWHAT TO COMPARE (per scenario, across methods):\n- winner (legacy fork_convergence / outcomes.winner), heal time, reorg count\n  and depth, blocks orphaned per camp\n- hashrate and economic-weight trajectories; price gap\n- time of the first pool switch (cascade timing)\n- real-fork only: outcomes per node, inplace_switching events and downtime\n\nEXPECTED DIFFERENCES THAT ARE FINDINGS, NOT FAILURES: propagation delay;\nreal wipe-out cycles (legacy counts mined blocks, observed oracles count\nsurviving ones); restart downtime and re-sync lag under inplace; bridged\ntopology (5 fork links) vs legacy's scripted bridging.\n\nNEXT STEP IF THE METHODS AGREE: sweep violation_rate (the threshold\nquestion) and fork_links under realfork/inplace.\n",
    "spec_file": "specs/knots_method_pilot.yaml",
